<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Exposure Problem Analysis</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In text-conditioned diffusion, a neural network <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to predict the noise at each timestep <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> given a noisy <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and a positive prompt embedding <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. By also training with <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by the null embedding <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>, the model acquires an unconditional predictor <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t},t,\varnothing)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t},t,\varnothing)</annotation></semantics></math>. At inference, Classifier-Free Guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans 2022</a>)</cite> interpolates these two estimates using a guidance scale <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\epsilon}_{\phi}(x_{t},t,c;\,\omega)=(1+\omega)\,\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t},t,c)-\omega\,\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t},t,\varnothing)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\epsilon}_{\phi}(x_{t},t,c;\,\omega)=(1+\omega)\,\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t},t,c)-\omega\,\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t},t,\varnothing)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=0</annotation></semantics></math> yields pure conditional sampling and larger <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> amplifies the conditional signal. This simple mechanism requires no auxiliary classifier, affords a smooth fidelity-diversity trade-off via <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, and has been instrumental in models such as Stable Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al. 2022a</a>)</cite>, Imagen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saharia et al. 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)</annotation></semantics></math> as a consistency model is trained to match the ODE trajectory in a single or few steps. Naively solving the diffusion ODE with pure conditional predictions <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)</annotation></semantics></math> leads to trajectories that deviate from the data manifold, yielding blurred or structurally inconsistent outputs. Hence, each step employs a CFG-augmented estimate that may also incorporate a null or negative embedding <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\mathrm{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\mathrm{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c,c_{n};\omega)&amp;=\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c_{n})\\
&amp;\quad+\omega\bigl(\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)-\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c_{n})\bigr)\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c,c_{n};\omega)&amp;=\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c_{n})\\
&amp;\quad+\omega\bigl(\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)-\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c_{n})\bigr)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">At inference, if we further apply CFG with scale <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to the consistency model itself. Denoting by <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}^{\omega^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}^{\omega^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> the state distilled under diversity scale <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, its noise prediction becomes:</p>
<table id="A1.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\epsilon}=(1+\omega)\,\epsilon_{\theta}\bigl(x_{t}^{\omega^{\prime}},t,c\bigr)-\omega\,\epsilon_{\theta}\bigl(x_{t}^{\omega^{\prime}},t,\varnothing\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\epsilon}=(1+\omega)\,\epsilon_{\theta}\bigl(x_{t}^{\omega^{\prime}},t,c\bigr)-\omega\,\epsilon_{\theta}\bigl(x_{t}^{\omega^{\prime}},t,\varnothing\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As theoretical analysis in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> shows that this compounds the consistency model: for all <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}\leq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}\leq t</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="A1.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c,c_{\mathrm{n}};\,\omega^{\prime})&amp;\propto\;\omega\,\omega^{\prime}\,\bigl(\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t^{\prime}},t^{\prime},c)-\epsilon_{\mathrm{m}}^{\phi}\bigr)\\
&amp;\quad\;+\;\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t^{\prime}},t^{\prime},c_{\mathrm{n}})\ \end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.838em">∝</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.280em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c,c_{\mathrm{n}};\,\omega^{\prime})&amp;\propto\;\omega\,\omega^{\prime}\,\bigl(\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t^{\prime}},t^{\prime},c)-\epsilon_{\mathrm{m}}^{\phi}\bigr)\\
&amp;\quad\;+\;\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t^{\prime}},t^{\prime},c_{\mathrm{n}})\ \end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\mathrm{m}}^{\phi}=(1-\alpha)\,\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t^{\prime}},t^{\prime},c_{\mathrm{n}})+\alpha\,\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t^{\prime}},t^{\prime},\varnothing)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\mathrm{m}}^{\phi}=(1-\alpha)\,\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t^{\prime}},t^{\prime},c_{\mathrm{n}})+\alpha\,\epsilon_{\phi}(x_{t^{\prime}},t^{\prime},\varnothing)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=(\omega-1)/(\omega\,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=(\omega-1)/(\omega\,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This is equivalent to scaling the predictions of the original diffusion model by a factor of <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\,\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\,\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which leads to severe exposure issues when using larger CFG values during inference. Thus, large CFG (e.g., CFG <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> 7) accentuates edges and contrast but suppresses texture complexity and flattens fine details, whereas setting CFG (e.g., CFG <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math> 1) too low causes the ODE path to drift off-manifold, producing blurry or semantically inconsistent samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, we observe that prior work has typically proposed its own solutions. For example, PCM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> trains and stores model weights for various values of <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> to accommodate subsequent applications with different <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for inference; TCD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. 2024</a>)</cite> provides weights that perform well when CFG is around 2.5 but, as our experiments show in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Adaptive Sampling ‣ 3 Method ‣ Adaptive Sampling Scheduler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.1 Backbones ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Adaptive Sampling Scheduler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, degrade severely when CFG <math id="A1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> 7; and TD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2025</a>)</cite> mitigates the exposure problem by fixing CFG at 3.5 during training, and using the clipping method proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saharia et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, yet the exposure issue still occurs when CFG (e.g. 7.5) is higher or when the number of inference steps is increased (e.g. to 8 steps).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, most diffusion models employ a CFG scale of 7.5 to achieve outputs that adhere more closely to the prompt. Therefore, we did not deliberately choose a smaller CFG value; instead, to remain consistent with real‐world usage, all of our experiments were conducted with CFG set to 7.5. Using our method, the severe overexposure issues induced by large CFG values (i.e. CFG <math id="A1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> 7) can be effectively mitigated.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Smooth clipping &amp; color balance</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When employing a high CFG scale (e.g. <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=7.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=7.5</annotation></semantics></math>) together with more inference steps (8 steps), most existing methods exhibit severe over‐exposure artifacts. Even applying the cropping strategy of prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saharia et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. 2022</a>)</cite> only partially mitigates the effect-the exposure problem persists.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In an over-exposed image, the vast majority of pixel magnitudes cluster near the maximum, so the quantile-based threshold:</p>
<table id="A1.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\mathrm{Clamp}\&gt;\bigl(\mathrm{quantile}_{\alpha}(|x|),\,1,\,V\bigr)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Clamp</mi><mo lspace="0.220em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>quantile</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\mathrm{Clamp}\&gt;\bigl(\mathrm{quantile}_{\alpha}(|x|),\,1,\,V\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">(with, e.g., <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.995" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.995</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.995</annotation></semantics></math>) almost always saturates at its upper bound <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. All Pixels are then normalized via follow:</p>
<table id="A1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{i}\;=\;\frac{\mathrm{clip}(x_{i},\,-s,\,s)}{s}\ " display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{i}\;=\;\frac{\mathrm{clip}(x_{i},\,-s,\,s)}{s}\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which, when <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=V</annotation></semantics></math>, reduces to <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{i}=\frac{x_{i}}{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>V</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{i}=\frac{x_{i}}{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under this mapping, even mid-tone values (e.g. <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\approx 0.5V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\approx 0.5V</annotation></semantics></math>) are confined to the narrow interval <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.5,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.5,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, collapsing the dynamic range, obliterating contrast, and flattening image details.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, our proposed <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and color balance scheme overcomes these issues in two ways. First, <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh(x)</annotation></semantics></math> enforces soft saturation it smoothly compresses all values into <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and only gradually attenuates extremes, rather than hard-clipping them. This preserves continuous saturation transitions and avoids abrupt clipping artifacts. Second, if the <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> deviates too much from reasonable area (e.g. exposure situation), most values will fall into the saturation zone (output tends to <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>1) as clamp in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saharia et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, so continue subtracting the per-channel and then the global mean recenter the data around reasonable area, maintaining relative brightness differences across regions without uniformly suppressing low-level and mid-level intensities through a single global threshold.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>More experimental results</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further demonstrate the effectiveness and robustness of our method, we have added additional visual comparisons in Figure <a href="#A1.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ A.3 More experimental results ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Adaptive Sampling Scheduler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. Here, we select the original form of the methods that performed better, PCM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2024</a>)</cite> and TDD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, and combined them with our method to clearly highlight the improvements of our solution in terms of detail preservation, exposure correction, and overall visual fidelity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12569v1/sup1.png" id="A1.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:698/925;" width="698" height="925" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Qualitative comparison between PCM, TDD and with Ours base on Stable Diffusion XL.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</article>
